<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cusco School</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cuzco School</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> School was a tradition of Catholic religious painting centred on Cusco in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> from the late sixteenth to the eighteenth century. Its largely indigenous and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizo</a> painters adapted European models, adding Andean plants, animals and dress, flattened perspective and lavish gold brocade. Its works, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> series of Cusco, were produced in large numbers for churches across the Andes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cusco-school.jpg" alt="Cusco School" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virgin_of_Carmel_Saving_Souls_in_Purgatory_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The tradition arose after the Spanish conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1534 and is considered the first artistic centre in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> to teach European techniques systematically. Religious artists sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> taught drawing and oil painting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">Mestizo</a> pupils, for the art's main purpose was didactic. The arrival of the Italian Jesuit painter Bernardo Bitti in 1583 brought Mannerism, with its elongated figures lit by focused light. Luis de Riaño, a disciple of Angelino Medoro, led local painting between 1618 and 1640, and Diego Cusihuamán painted murals in the churches of Chinchero and Urcos.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> influence came through the tenebrism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-zurbaran">Francisco de Zurbarán</a> and engravings of Flemish art from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, above all in the work of Marcos Ribera, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> in 1630. Towards the end of the 17th century the growing number of Amerindian-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">Mestizo</a> painters gave the school its own character. In 1688, after continuous conflicts, the painters' corporation split and the Amerindian and Mestizo painters left it, citing exploitation by their Spanish colleagues, and from then on they followed their own sensibility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Cusqueña painting kept to religious subjects, with little perspective, space broken into several compartmentalised scenes, a predominance of red, yellow and earth colours and lavish gold leaf, especially on images of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>. Its painters added Andean flora and fauna, warrior angels, portraits of Amerindian caciques and scenes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> procession. Most works are anonymous. Diego Quispe Tito, born in San Sebastián near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> in 1611, is regarded as its most original Amerindian painter, and the sixteen canvases of the Corpus Christi series, from the late 17th century, show the social strata of colonial Cusco in detail.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the 18th century workshops made canvases in large quantities for merchants who sold them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arequipa">Arequipa</a> and other cities and as far as present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>; Mauricio García once contracted to deliver about five hundred in seven months. Marcos Zapata (c. 1710–1773), the leading artist of that century, painted more than 200 works between 1748 and 1764. The largest collection of the school's paintings is in the Cusco Cathedral, and the Lima Art Museum and the Inca Museum also hold important ones.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cusco school” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Colonial Peru also produced classic accounts of the Andean past, the Royal Commentaries of the Incas by Inca Garcilaso de la Vega, published in 1609, and the illustrated chronicle of Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala, while the <b>Cuzco School</b> of painting blended European and Andean styles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
